## Expanding Structs with More Information

So far we've used two-field structs. But real data is richer — a book has a title, an author, AND a page count.

### Defining a Book Struct

```xiom
type Book = {
  title: Str;
  author: Str;
  pages: Int;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let book1 = Book{ title: "The Hobbit", author: "J.R.R. Tolkien", pages: 310 };
  let book2 = Book{ title: "Dune", author: "Frank Herbert", pages: 412 };
  let book3 = Book{ title: "Ender's Game", author: "Orson Scott Card", pages: 324 };

  io.println(book1.title + " by " + book1.author);
  io.println(book2.title + " by " + book2.author);
  io.println(book3.title + " by " + book3.author);
  return 0;
}
```

### Different Field Types

Fields can be any type you've learned — `Str`, `Int`, `Float64`, `Bool`:

```xiom
type Movie = {
  title: Str;
  year: Int;
  rating: Float64;
  is_favorite: Bool;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let movie = Movie{ title: "Wall-E", year: 2008, rating: 8.4, is_favorite: true };
  io.println(movie.title);
  io.println(movie.is_favorite);
  return 0;
}
```

### Structs with Many Fields

There's no artificial limit. If your data needs 5, 10, or even 20 fields — add them. The struct keeps them organized together under one name.

